WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
An SBOM can be syntactically valid and still not useful for the question at hand.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.680
Start by confirming that it identifies the right application, package, image, service, or release.

3
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.220
Confirm the format and version are acceptable for intended consumers.

4
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:33.440
Check whether direct and transitive dependencies are represented, whether dependency relationships are understandable, and whether the SBOM includes enough package identifiers, versions, purls, hashes, licenses, supplier metadata, and tool metadata to support real response work.

5
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.880
Completeness depends on purpose.

6
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:42.620
A production runtime SBOM that includes every development-only test library may create noise for runtime triage.

7
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:55.920
A runtime SBOM that omits bundled code, workspace packages, optional dependencies, peer dependencies, native modules, generated artifacts, or container packages may miss important risk.

8
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.340
The answer is not to include or exclude categories blindly.

9
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:08.920
The answer is to define scope, check that the output matches that scope, and document known omissions rather than pretending the SBOM is complete for every question.

10
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:22.720
Compare SBOM output against package-lock.json, installed packages, workspace manifests, container scans, dependency dashboards, build logs, and release metadata where appropriate.

11
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:30.820
Ask three operational questions: complete for what purpose, generated from what input, and tied to which artifact.

12
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:38.680
Quality checks make SBOMs useful during incidents because responders can understand what the document can answer, what it cannot answer, and where to look next.
